Subject: COSMO: Holographic Universe Theory

[perfectly valid criticisms deleted for space - good points all]

>   Does the holographic model make any testable predictions or is it yet
> another pseudo-religous model? It sounds a lot like the Buddism-Newage
> speculations in the last chapter of "The Dancing Wu Li Masters". 

Testable in what manner?  I'd say H.U.T. is as testable as QM.  I.e. some
of its effects can be seen and quantified, but the core of the theory is
based on math, and well a little bit of faith.  I'd ask if this is a flaw
with the theory, or with these types of theories (including relativity,
etc.), in general, not to mention nanotech.  As Eric Drexler states, as 
more advanced scientific memes evolve, the more they evolve to BE true,
not to SEEM true, and often are counter-intuitive at first blush.  And as
I believe Clarke put it [paraphrase] "Any sufficiently advanced
technology is indistinguishable from magic" [or was that Heinlein? I
forget].  Anyway, I'd have to say yes, H.U.T. and related thinking is not
directly provable, but what is really?  More importantly, it does not
seem to be directly DISprovable. This does not make it true, but it does
make it just as viable a theory as any other cosmology. To the original
researchers' credit, I have yet to see any of them get "newagey" or
religious about it.  This sort of stuff (not NECESSARILY clap-trap; I
think it is a good thing to be looking for explanations of what has
heretofore been unexplainable -- that's part of what science is all about)
seems to be purely the product of popularizers like Talbot.  I think they
mean well, but as I said when I first posted about this, I find their
expansions of H.U.T. to be clouded and fruity, but I'd like to see more
serious science on the matter before I throw the baby out w/the bathwater.
And I am personally very skeptical about those who automatically toss out
unusual ideas because they don't seem to fit at first or are too "far
out".  That Newton, Gallileo, Einstein, Heisenberg, Shroedinger, etc.
all met such opposition is a bit of a caveat. 

