Date: Tue, 8 Jun 1993 02:04:41 -0600 (MDT)
From: Stanton McCandlish <anton@hydra.unm.edu>
Subject: COSMO: Holographic Universe Theory

Quoth Michael Clive Price, verily I saith unto thee:

> The basic premise of holographic universe 'theory' is everything's
> interconnected.  'Fraid this flies right in the face of relativity which
> limits all communications, interactions etc to the speed of light. 
> According to relativity, which is experimentally very well verified,
> influences have a limiting speed.  According to HUT there is no limiting
> speed.

Hmm well I am no PhD'd physicist, but as I understand both HUT and
Relativity, there is no  conflict.  HUT does not redifine any of the
operating parameters of the apprehendable universe.  It simply says that
in the implicate realm, which would have little if anything to do with
Relativity or any other physics that attempt to define what's going on in
the explicate realm, one can have an interaction of "waves" that manifests
in more than one way, place, time, effect, etc in the explicate realm.  
As a (likely quite poor, but still pretty clear) analogy, take a rock and
drop it in a pool of water. you get an expanding ring of ripples.  Now
look at this in x-section

                          | >plop<
                          |
________/\_______/\___/\_\|/_/\___/\_______/\________
 
^^                                         ^^  
flat surface                                wave

To something that cannot perceive the entire structure [shades of Flatland
here] what you have is a bunch of "events" (the wave crests) that seems
totally separate.  Lets say our limited-POV frog or whatever decides to
try to affect the course of these "events" and distrupts one wave.  All
sorts of repercussions may come back as waves interact, and Kermit will
wonder "what the hell happened...wierd <shudder> must be a
[synchronicity|miracle|UFO ray gun|other mumbojumbo].  However if Kermy
could see the whole wave, it'd know that each of the "events" is in fact
part of one interconnected structure of events, and that fiddling with
part of it may affect the whole thing, as waves interact with other waves.
Something about the butterfly effect and how we can't see hypercubes would
likely be relevant here, but I don't have the background for it yet.

If we look at that pic up there as a general [if cheezy] model of how
matter is manifested, you could see the points of the waves as, say,
physical locations.  Sure relativity says the most-left crest can't
communicate with the most-right (or any other) crest, except at sublight
speeds.  But looking at how the wave is really manifested, by the
interactions of properties of the implicate realm we can see that not only
is every wave crest "communicating" they are all part of the same phenomenon >
and extend in another dimension entirely that we do not even see. In fact
the left-most crest is actually the SAME "thing" as the the right-most one.
This may be relevant to wormhole theories, and even to something I read
long ago about how quasars in pairs seemed to be "communicating" instantly.
I am sure there are other theories for the effect, but again the
terminology and such exceeds my current state of learning in physics and
cosmology, unfortunately.  Anyway, suppose that all this talk of jumping
into hyperspace, and using traversable wormholes, is just a way of
utilizing implicate structures, rather than explicate ones.

Oh, and also, it should be noted that the formulators of HUT stress that
it is not meant to be taken literally as "waves" but that the term is just
a metaphor, and something like it is needed because we CAN'T directly
apprehend the implicate.  It's just a model as Patsy would say.  Or "The
Tao that can be spoken of is not the eternal Tao".

Another probably-not-so-minor point is that, as far as I can tell, HUT is
not saying "everything is interconnected ohm ohm ohm" but that events,
forces, physical items, ideas, etc. MAY be connected.  Thus the appeal of
trying to explain archetypes, synchronicity, simultaneous inventions,
morphic theory, etc by recourse to HUT.  Perhaps it's misguided, perhaps
it's right on the mark. Who's to say this early in the game?

> If Holographic
Universe Theory is RIGHT, well the implications > > are ENORMOUS.
> 
> Which is a reason to be sceptical.  Doesn't mean it's necessarily
> rubbish, but I think we have the right to see definite mathematical
> models and predictions before taking it seriously.

Certainly!  This is one of the reasons I brought it up.  I was hoping
someone had come across more info and research since Bohm's initial
publications, and those of Pribram, etc. [BTW, the Bohm book that another
poster cited is the main source of Talbot's book].
 
> When I see an instantaneous communicator, or detailed plans for one,
> built using HUT, I'll believe it.

Well, I think the same flaw in understanding that yielded the Relativity
objection is at work here.  To do that you'd need the telecom equivalent
of a Star Trek|Wars warp or hyperspace mechanism: something to get you (or
the device or whatever) outside the explicate realm and into, or in someway
more directly patched into, the implicate realm. 

> As far as I can see HUT/implicate order is all based on miconceptions
> about quantum mechanics.  Widely shared misconceptions, to be sure, but
> misconceptions nonetheless.

Interesting.  Lets have it.  I'd like to know more about what you see in
this vein.  Somehow I'm skeptical, since I have yet to encounter any of his
peers accusing Bohm of that, but still.  How do you see this as
misconceiving QM?  

Thanks for the response, glad of the opportunity to discuss this.  I am
not all for HUT, I just think it is an interesting twist, and IF it won't
hold up, I'd like to know why, and IF it will, I want to know why.  IF it
is unprovable, and thus 'not important' or whatever, it still stands to
reason that it is just as good as any other such theory, so by definition
it's still important.  :)

