cooking.  During the experiment, "the diet" became the central fact of
my life, and the time factor became rather frustrating.  
(Okay, so I'm spoiled (and lazy) :-)

  I'd like to try another experiment in the near future, but this time with 
as many time-saving practices as possible.  Walford recommends getting a food 
processor for cutting vegetables. I didn't listen the first time, but now 
I realize that it's probably an excellent idea.

Perry Metzger wrote in response to a statement:
|> There's nothing finer than bread with a vegetable-laden thick pea soup, 
|> but it's a major project.
|
| If you live alone, you can cook up a very large batch and freeze most
| of it in small portions suitable to defrost every week or so.

  This is also an excellent idea.  For the Walford Diet (which has a wide 
variety of dishes), this probably means I'll need to buy a small freezer;
the space I have now is just too small.

  Another idea is to take advantage of Pritikin products (soups, bread,
mixes), since they are fairly convenient and are high-quality foods.
Walford mentions in one of his books that the Pritikin program is 
generally excellent, even though it doesn't balance nutrients precisely.

  Here's an idea for budding extropreneurs:  Pritikin / Walford consistent 
nutrient-balanced frozen dinners!  If such were available, and at reasonable 
prices, I'd be living on them now.  All I'd have to do would be to combine
them with occassional food-processed fresh vegetable side dishes, and I'd 
be set.  I wonder how many others would find this solution appealing?


